   "rule": {
    "title": "Names you already know",
    "body": "Names borrowed from Hebrew often do not decline at all (Ἰσραήλ, Ἀβραάμ, Δαυίδ); the article shows their case. Ἰησοῦς has its own tiny paradigm: Ἰησοῦς, Ἰησοῦ, Ἰησοῦ, Ἰησοῦν. Jerusalem appears in two spellings, Ἰερουσαλήμ (indeclinable) and Ἱεροσόλυμα (neuter plural).",
    "examples": [
     {
      "gr": "Ἰησοῦς Χριστός",
      "en": "Jesus Christ"
     },
